technical_components.what:
Changes to app.py and animate.py:
- multi-trial tables;
- an uncertainty-normalised agreement meter that replaces the 15% rule;
- a suspect board with live posterior bars;
- ghost overlays;
- a next-measurement card that shows each suspect's predicted outcome before the student measures;
- a verdict with an interval;
- a fix-and-verify loop;
- scripted mode, teacher settings and sabotage mode;
- a booth Judge Challenge with 12 paper sabotage cards.
The fault-parameterised animation of 'the experiment you actually ran' is the first item to cut if time runs short.

scores.justification:
Best-controlled ML evaluation of the four. Every arm gets the same contract prompt and signature panel. Controls include a shuffled-label LoRA negative control (H7), a gradient-boosted-tree baseline that asks whether an LLM is needed at all, oracle-in-prompt and planted-duplicate positive controls, TOST equivalence tests for what must not change, non-inferiority against the 27B, and Mac/server parity with an adapter-effect check. The 9B bf16 LoRA (about 22 GB per the verified Unsloth table) avoids the QLoRA caveat and is a credible upgrade.

It overreaches. The plan has 24 families with 3-4 faults each, including Ohm, Snell, Boyle, resonance tube, calorimetry and internal resistance, all AI-written in five days. Only six are physics-checked before SFT. Generator and referee share code, so a physics bug is self-consistent and invisible to every metric, and no student can defend 24 fault models in a 15-minute interview.

All confirmatory claims stay inside the simulator:
- transfer rests on only 6 held-out clusters;
- accuracy is scored only on items the referee calls identifiable;
- there is no world with misspecified physics;
- the only real-data test (E8) is exploratory and may be empty by 11-15.

The claim that a physics engine as the only teacher is new is contradicted by Sim2Reason and PhysEvolve. Heavy runs are not assigned to the student, which risks counting as AI collecting data, and the Mac model downloads start on 11-03.

technical_components.what:
- Monte Carlo Bayes-optimal confusion matrices for each of the 12 experiments under these protocols:
  - P0, single reading (the old app);
  - P1, the standard multi-point worksheet as written in the student's textbook (he confirms it);
  - P1 plus 1 EIG measurement, and P1 plus 2;
  - controls: P1 plus 1 repeat, and P1 plus 1 random item.
- Outputs: pairwise confusability; the measurement that resolves each confused pair; sensitivity to fault-size priors and noise (x0.5, x2).
- It also provides the oracle ceiling that every model arm is normalised against, and the teacher's protocol view.
- Worked examples the student can derive by hand:
  - Energy, with only the release height varied at one angle: four causes all give a constant KE/PE ratio below 1. They are slant length used as height (ratio sin theta), rolling friction (1 - mu cot theta), rotational energy (5/7, lower on two rails) and a flag-width error (k^2). One change of angle separates the first two from the last two; measuring the ball and the flag separates the last two.
  - Hooke: a hanger mass left out of the force, a ruler zero offset and a recorded total length all appear as a nonzero intercept of the F-x line, because with a free slope an x-offset is indistinguishable from a y-offset. Only their typical sizes differ. The atlas measures how often the worksheet confuses them and names the separating measurement (weigh the hanger; read the unloaded spring).

- Code computes the panel for any experiment card (law in linearised form y = f(x), variables, units, instrument resolutions) and data table:
  - fit of the ideal law with a free scale: slope ratio to ideal and its z;
  - intercept z;
  - curvature z (quadratic term);
  - residual scatter against expected instrument noise (chi^2/dof, too large or too small);
  - runs test and drift against trial order;
  - for conservation experiments, the before/after ratio with its uncertainty.
- The same vocabulary is used by the engine's cause tags, the model's output and the UI's suspect cards.
- Every arm (base, tuned, 27B, API, classical) receives the identical panel, so a gain cannot come from the fine-tune learning arithmetic. A no-panel ablation measures what the panel itself contributes.
- In the open-set product path the same code analyses any experiment once the student picks its relation family.

technical_components.novelty_vs_prior_art:
Simulator labels and rewards already exist for forward physics QA (Sim2Reason 2026), VLM world-building (PhysEvolve 2026) and physics code (RLVP 2026). Calibration-aware rewards (RLCR) and STaR/RFT are known. New here:
- the inverse task: the cause from noisy, student-style measurements;
- the engine posterior as a proper-scoring target;
- measurement design as an output;
- transfer measured on held-out experiments as a fraction of the Bayes-optimal oracle.
It also tests BoxingGym's finding within one domain.

technical_components.what:
- Extends the existing Streamlit app. YOLO, the correction canvas and manual selection of the six experiments stay.
- explain_api.py (the GLM API) is removed. A local client talks to mlx_vlm.server, which provides JSON schema output, logprobs and thinking off by default; an automatic check confirms that no reasoning text comes back.
- intent_classifier.py keeps its rules; the local VLM replaces the GLM fallback.
- New: multi-point tables with instrument profiles, the agreement meter, the suspect board, ghost curves, the next-measurement card with predicted outcomes, an optional predict-first step, the verdict with fix-and-verify, scripted mode, and the open-set 'Other experiment' path.
- Laptop tier only (Qwen3.5-4B plus the adapter). The 27B never runs alongside the app.
- Watchdog (psutil, polling every 2 s): sums the footprint of every process we start and logs the peak. Above [21] GB it refuses to start new jobs. Above [23] GB it stops the simplest task first, in this order:
  - the browser preview tab;
  - CPU batch jobs (checkpointed);
  - the eval client (checkpointed per item);
  - the YOLO worker (fallback: manual apparatus entry);
  - the in-flight LLM request (fallback: template);
  - the model server, last.

- The existing rule classifier runs first.
- On ties or 'other', the local 4B sees the photo (<= 1024 px) with numbered YOLO boxes (Set-of-Mark) and fills a JSON schema:
  - single-letter codes, A-F for the six experiments and G for other, so token logprobs are clean;
  - an open-vocabulary name;
  - configuration cues (hanging mass over a pulley, two carts facing each other, tilted track, horizontal dynamometer);
  - the apparatus list.
- The proven cart+track tie is settled by one deterministic question, 'What will you measure?'. When cues are only partial, the question is chosen by EIG.
- Split-conformal candidate sets, calibrated on source-grouped splits. They are reported per source group as small-n and never described to users as 'guaranteed'.
- Minimal pairs from existing boxes: inpaint the decisive apparatus (OpenCV Telea) and, as a matched-artifact control, apply the same mask and fill to background.
- Data hygiene: person screening with the COCO yolov8n.pt already in the project folder; pHash de-duplication; splits grouped by EXIF session or source video.

Phase 2 (Dec 2026 - Feb 2027):
- S3 GRPO, if not already run;
- a Qwen3.5-9B scale point;
- more real runs (toward about 60) with the same method;
- a vision-side counterfactual LoRA on minimal pairs with source-grouped splits;
- a sympy law compiler with a dimensional check for unregistered experiments;
- the 27B on the full test set via llama.cpp GGUF on the server.
Phase 3 (conditional, not before Dec 2026): the IRB-approved classroom study (E9).

experiments.data:
Frozen synthetic splits:
- in-distribution: 600 (100 per curriculum experiment);
- noise-shift: 300 (Student-t errors with nu = 3, doubled timing sigma, coarser resolution);
- withheld-cause: 300;
- clean: 150;
- unmodelled: 150 (two simultaneous causes; a x10 transcription slip);
- hi-fi misspecified: 300.
The 6 held-out engines act as graders. External realism check: the simulated spread of pendulum-g estimates against a published student distribution (SD 0.28 m/s^2 over 271 values), which the student must locate and read himself before using it.

Frozen test sets, with hashes in the signed plan before training:
- in-distribution: 600;
- noise-shift: 300;
- withheld-cause: 300;
- held-out experiments: 720 (6 x 120), plus 120 cause-proposal tasks;
- hi-fi: 300;
- clean: 150;
- unmodelled: 150;
- swapped-table control: 150;
- oracle-in-prompt: 100;
- closed-loop single-step decisions: 600.
A separate 300-case in-distribution validation split is used only for early stopping and the deployment rule.

experiments.metrics_and_stats:
- Primary: top-1 cause accuracy on the 720 held-out cases, S1 vs base, exact McNemar. Also reported as a fraction of the oracle, with an experiment-level cluster bootstrap CI. Valid only if the shuffled-label control gains < [2] pp.
- Secondary (Holm):
  - the ladder (in-distribution, noise-shift, withheld-cause, hi-fi);
  - S2 vs S1;
  - EIG-regret (Wilcoxon);
  - Brier, log score, ECE;
  - signature-type accuracy for the candidate causes;
  - recall@6 of proposed causes, matched automatically on variable and signature type;
  - false alarms;
  - non-inferiority vs the 27B (margin [3] pp).
- Also reported:
  - per-experiment and near- vs far-transfer breakdowns (classified in advance: near = inclined-plane friction, Atwood, free fall; far = pendulum, projectile, lever);
  - mixed-effects logistic regression with an experiment random intercept, as a sensitivity analysis;
  - JSON validity, latency and peak memory.

experiments.human_participants_status:
Exempt as long as:
- the student is the only person who measures and uses the software;
- no information about anyone else is recorded;
- no human variable is studied (no reaction-time manipulation; timing faults are simulated only).
An adult may be present only for safety supervision, and this is written into the plan. Confirm with the SRC in writing. Form 3 covers masses, springs and carts.

Human-participant research. Required before any recruitment:
- Form 4 signed by an educator who is not the sponsor, a school administrator, and a medical or mental-health professional;
- written minor assent plus parental permission, with any worksheet or survey attached;
- adult consent;
- de-identification by an adult;
- template-only outputs;
- a written IRB/SRC ruling on showing app output to participants, given the Companion Guide's advice.
The feature is called 'error-source analysis' in every document. If the study is not approved, it is not run, and nothing from any classroom appears anywhere in the project.

risks:
Plan or signatures slip past 11-06, so every formal run waits. Mitigation:
- all tooling is push-button by 11-05;
- the 4B jobs take hours, so a one-day slip still fits;
- P1 items are dropped first, never the controls;
- the variant schedule.

The Qwen3.5 training stack fails on Blackwell sm_120 (Gated DeltaNet kernels, flash-linear-attention, causal-conv1d). Mitigation:
- the environment is set up on 11-06 with no data;
- fallback chain Unsloth -> ms-swift -> PEFT/TRL;
- Mac-native mlx-vlm SFT of the 4B overnight on reduced data;
- v1 can ship with the untuned 4B plus the engine.

No transfer: C3 fails. Mitigation:
- the claim is pre-registered, so a miss is reportable;
- the ladder shows where the gain stops;
- the controls and the signature-type metric explain why;
- the atlas, engine and closed loop stand on their own;
- Phase 2 adds S3 and the 9B.

Physics bugs in AI-written fault models would be invisible to every metric, because the generator and the engine share code. Mitigation:
- mechanics-only scope;
- the student reviews the physics cards before training;
- analytic unit tests;
- independent re-implementation by the second assistant;
- the student's hand-coded fault model;
- the real runs;
- any correction goes into the Project Addendum and triggers a retrain.

C1 is only as good as the fault library, so it risks being true by construction. Mitigation:
- causes come from the curriculum and teacher literature, or are flagged 'assumption';
- the library is frozen by hash before the atlas runs;
- sensitivity analysis;
- one predicted confound is tested on real apparatus.

Shortcut learning on synthetic data (ID positions, template strings, magnitude ranges). Mitigation:
- randomised IDs and order, paraphrases, overlapping priors;
- noise-shift and hi-fi splits;
- the swapped-table control and the leakage checker.

The adapter conversion is silently wrong, or the bf16 -> MLX 4-bit gap degrades results. Mitigation:
- converter tests;
- LoRA only on modules with one-to-one names;
- the adapter-effect check and the 200-case parity gate;
- the fuse or Mac-native training fallbacks.

Sim-to-real gap, or a misspecified engine. Mitigation:
- literature-calibrated noise;
- the hi-fi world;
- real runs and realism checks;
- claims labelled 'under our simulator's assumptions' until real data exist.

Breaching the 24 GB cap on the Mac. Mitigation:
- the product runs on the 4B (about 6-8 GB);
- the 27B runs only alone, overnight;
- the watchdog with a fixed kill order;
- images <= 1024 px and an 8k context.

Runtime quirks: thinking text leaking into replies, invalid JSON, missing logprobs. Mitigation:
- mlx_vlm.server with thinking off by default, plus an automatic no-reasoning check;
- schema-constrained decoding and the template fallback;
- sampling-frequency scores if logprobs fail.

ISEF integrity failure: an AI-drafted plan or abstract, AI-supplied citations, AI-collected data, unapproved human participants, or an undeclared continuation. Mitigation:
- the rules in this design;
- the ledger and the prompt logs;
- student-launched runs;
- SRC rulings obtained in writing.

Recognition results are fragile: the images are small and clustered (3 source videos), and their provenance is unclear. Mitigation:
- recognition is P1 and evaluation-only;
- results reported per source as small-n, with no guarantee claims;
- the CC set serves as the independent test;
- uncleared frames are excluded.

Student workload in 9 days. Mitigation:
- a P0-only critical path;
- the briefing pack;
- real runs capped at 24;
- the variant schedule;
- the assistants handle all code under the ledger.

Judges ask 'why train a model if the engine is exact?' and challenge the novelty. Mitigation:
- engines exist only for modelled experiments, and the held-out design measures exactly the skill that is deployed;
- a prior-art map marks what is new;
- the student re-checks CNKI and CNIPA.

The 4B may be weak at recognition. Mitigation:
- rules run first, with one deterministic question;
- manual selection is always available;
- E7 measures the 4B against the 27B;
- a vision-side upgrade is listed for Phase 2;
- a second base-model server is used if the TOST fails.

decision_log:
Fatal flaw fixed (S2 sampled from the untuned base, with an unknown acceptance rate):
- the primary arm is now S1 (engine-target SFT, near-certain to exist by 11-08) vs base;
- S2 becomes expert iteration seeded from merged S1 (P1);
- a pre-registered deployment rule picks S1 or S2 for the product.

Hypotheses compressed from H1-H8 plus EC1-EC6 to three confirmatory claims:
- C1, the atlas, reframed around the standard worksheet; the single-reading clause becomes descriptive only;
- C2, EIG beats repeat, in simulation (hi-fi world) and in real runs;
- C3, S1 beats base on held-out experiments.
Everything else is a Holm-corrected secondary, a pass/fail control or an engineering criterion.

Controls:
- the lead's H7 row shuffle is replaced by a swapped-table control, since row order carries no information;
- positive controls added: oracle-in-prompt, planted duplicate, adapter-effect check;
- negative control added: a shuffled-label LoRA that must gain < 2 pp.

Real runs:
- BOTH the EIG follow-up and a plain repeat in every run, in randomised order (paired test);
- capped at 24 before 11-14, extended in Phase 2;
- sealed cards drawn by the student himself;
- no reaction-time manipulation, and stopwatch-only setups avoided;
- the spring-scale zero shift is measured;
- fix-and-verify is exploratory.

Wording fixes:
- 'Bayes-optimal under our simulator's assumptions' replaces 'exact';
- 'error-source analysis' in IRB documents;
- users never see 'guaranteed' coverage.

Conflict resolved: the ISEF judge cut 'Too-Perfect Twin' as an implied accusation, while the teacher wanted a too-perfect-data suspect. The resolution:
- no accusation-style suspect;
- the energy experiment gets a documented method-error check ('speed computed from the height', circular per teacher notes), off by default and enabled by the teacher;
- 'scatter smaller than instruments allow' stays as a neutral data signature.

Classroom from 11-07:
- ordinary teaching run by the teacher with v0.9 in scripted mode;
- nothing logged, collected, observed or presented, and no design change drawn from it;
- the IRB classroom study is listed as a conditional Phase 3;
- model-depth's anonymous cause-count export is cut.

Recognition:
- rules first, with a Set-of-Mark VLM fallback;
- one deterministic 'What will you measure?' question for the cart+track tie;
- conformal sets and minimal pairs with matched-artifact controls as P1 evaluation only.
CounterLab's CET training and its in-domain coverage guarantee are rejected, since the in-domain images come from only 3 source videos.

Student's open-set wish:
- P0: an 'Other experiment' path in which the student picks the relation family, code computes the generic panel, and the model's estimate is clearly labelled;
- P1: the VLM drafts the experiment card;
- Phase 2: the sympy law compiler.

questions_for_student:
Between 11-07 and 11-12, can you work alone in a lab, with an adult present only for safety? On which days, and for how many hours? Which apparatus is there:
- springs with slotted masses and a ruler;
- spring scales (what division and range?);
- block and board;
- cart, track and pulley;
- a photogate or ticker timer, or only stopwatches?
(This decides the 3 real-run experiments, and whether claim C2(b) can be tested before 11-15.)

problems.issue:
Some numbers the design states as fact come only from abstracts or search snippets, or from populations unlike the students:
- Kregear: 'more than 1 in 10 answers harmful';
- Maus: 'about 20% of feedback with errors';
- Faux & Godolphin's timing errors, measured on trained swimming timekeepers;
- Straulino's SD of 0.28 m/s^2, from first-year university students.
The realism check against Straulino means nothing unless the simulator copies his protocol.

go_no_go:
S1 engine-target SFT plus shuffled-label control, E4/C3 (P0): GO with the corrected design:
- S1 vs control is the gating test;
- the 3-shot base is the base comparator;
- a thinking-on base is added as a reference arm;
- top-1 is scored on equivalence classes;
- a per-experiment sign test is reported;
- a procedural task family is added to the training pool.
A null result is declared in advance as plausible.

Evaluation harness (P0): GO with these fixes:
- frozen test files are generated once on the Mac and uploaded (about 2 MB);
- chance is defined as the product of the marginals;
- K3 uses n of at least 600;
- truncation is reported per arm;
- an exact sign test across experiments replaces the 6-cluster bootstrap as the generalisation interval.

Product integration, local serving and watchdog (P0): GO, with:
- a watchdog based on phys_footprint;
- admission control that adds each job's declared peak;
- MLX memory and cache limits;
- HF_HUB_OFFLINE=1;
- Bionic/LM Studio quit and JIT off on baseline nights;
- the EC2 threshold set from a development measurement.

Real fault-injection kit, E5/C2b (P0): CONDITIONAL GO. It needs:
- confirmed lab access for two sessions before 11-12;
- adult-set sealed faults where possible;
- the fixed-extension follow-up;
- one held-out experiment run through 'Other experiment' with phone-sensor timing;
- no destructive elastic-limit fault.
Otherwise it stays 'pending' and runs in Phase 2 with the same method.
